## What this track is graded on (write the plan that wins these points)
A scene scores 0.8+ only when every authored camera makes a PICTURE: three depth layers
(something within 5 m framing the shot, the subject at 8-30 m, a hazy horizon above), ground
that is several blended materials with paths and worn edges, vegetation/rock in 3+ species with
size and hue variation in clusters, a lived-in amount of small props, coloured atmosphere with a
warm sun under a cool sky, and motion you can see between t=0 s and t=1.5 s — and NOTHING floating,
sunken or passing through its neighbour (the placement gate measures every placed thing and caps
the score).  A correct-but-bare scene — a diorama of a few objects on one flat coloured plane,
ringed by identical cones, under flat light — scores ~0.5 no matter how well it is coded.  Your
plan must make the bare version impossible: put the counts, the layers, the colours and the
amplitudes in the text.

WHAT TO PRODUCE (JSON, fields: {{ schema_fields }}):
1. `title`, `summary` (two sentences), `setting` (place, era, weather, time of day), `mood`.
2. `bounds` — centre + extents in meters of the whole playable area (a garden 30 x 10 x 30 m; a
   harbour 120 x 30 x 120 m). Keep it as small as the brief allows: detail beats acreage.
3. `environment` — ONE paragraph, all numbers, covering IN THIS ORDER:
   (a) the sun / moon azimuth (compass: 0 = +X, 90 = +Y) + elevation in degrees and the time of day
       (dawn / morning / noon / golden / overcast / dusk / night — the builders' lighting table);
   (b) the Sun lamp's colour + energy in W/m² (day 3-5, golden 2-2.5, dusk 1-1.5, a moon 0.1-0.3)
       AND the sky world strength (day 0.4-0.5, dusk 0.2, night 0.1) — a warm sun under a cool
       physical sky, or the frame comes out monochrome;
   (c) the fog: density (0.003-0.008) + colour (= the sky HORIZON colour, never white or grey unless
       overcast), as a bounded volume over the whole ground;
   (d) ground: the terrain stays within about +/-1.5 m of y = 0 inside the bounds (camera heights
       below are absolute, and a lifted floor puts them underground); ponds, terraces and gravel
       beds are carved INTO the terrain, never laid on it as flat discs; the 3-4 material colours
       that blend across it (e.g. moss (0.29, 0.38, 0.18), dry grass (0.54, 0.51, 0.28), packed
       dirt (0.42, 0.33, 0.20), gravel (0.66, 0.60, 0.49)), what drives the blend (height, slope,
       noise), and where the PATH runs as a polyline of 3-5 (x, z) points, 1.2-2.0 m wide;
   (e) the BACKDROP that hides the world edge: a sky-named silhouette ring (hills / treeline /
       rooftops / mesas) at radius ~ 0.6 x the visible range, 24-40 SOLID pieces (never flat
       cut-out planes) in their own colour darkened 0.6-0.8x — the fog supplies the haze, so never
       tint them toward the fog colour — or, for an interior, what is seen through the glass;
   (f) water plane height + colour, if any.
4. `zones` — 3 to 6 spatial regions with PascalCase names, a `bbox`, and `contents` = asset names
   placed there. Zone bboxes tile the bounds without overlapping by more than 1 m.
   Each zone `description` MUST state, as numbers:
   - its DEPTH ROLE for the cameras: `foreground frame`, `midground subject` or `background`;
   - the layout (paths, clusters, spacing in meters, what faces what, what stands ON what);
   - DENSITY COUNTS: how many trees / shrubs / rocks / mid props (0.3-1.5 m) / small props
     (< 0.3 m) / ground-cover blades it contains.  Target per zone: 12-25 mid props, 25-60 small
     props, 300-800 ground-cover blades for a planted zone (desert: 15-30 boulders in 3 size
     bands, 60-200 scrub clumps; interior: 15-30 mid, 40-100 small);
   - the VARIATION rule: at least 3 distinct silhouettes for any repeated natural element, with
     +/-30 % scale jitter and hue jitter — never one shape stamped in a ring or grid.
5. `assets` — 4 to 8 reusable things placed in zones. PascalCase `name`; `kind` = `bpy` for
   procedural props (fences, lanterns, benches, boats, trees the library does not grow) built as a
   bpy module and placed as collection instances, or `blender_glb` ONLY for 1-2 hero objects that
   need sculpted/bevelled detail (a hero may also carry ONE 2 s keyframed motion — a swinging lamp,
   a turning wheel — say so in its description); `description` with construction numbers and
   colours; `approx_size_m` = width x height x depth in meters (a stone lantern 0.5 x 1.6 x 0.5; a
   bench 1.6 x 0.9 x 0.6; a pine 3 x 7 x 3); `instances_hint` = how many appear in total.  Prefer
   FEWER asset modules with a `variant` range (say "5 variants via variant: three trunk shapes, two
   crown shapes") over many near-duplicate modules — variation belongs inside an asset, not in the
   asset list.  Ground cover, terrain, water, backdrop and sky are NOT assets — they belong to the
   environment / zones.
6. `effects` — 1 to 4: `glsl_material` (a shader-node effect: water ripple, lantern glow, a
   translucent leaf), `particles` (petals, sparks, snow — a small keyframed instance cloud),
   `animated_geometry`, or `postprocess` (a volume: mist, light shafts); say which zone/asset.
7. `animation` — 2 to 6 lines of what moves, each with an amplitude that is VISIBLE in a
   1024x576 frame after 1.5 s: foliage sway +/-0.10-0.20 rad; grass +/-0.15 rad; water ripples
   scrolling >= 0.15 m/s plus a bob >= 0.04 m; particles travelling >= 0.6 m per 1.5 s;
   vehicles/boats/sails >= 1.5 m or a visible turn per 1.5 s; lantern flicker x0.75-x1.3.
   Exactly one of them is the HERO motion: the single thing a viewer cannot miss.
8. `cameras` — 3 to 4 authored cameras.  The harness uses your numbers LITERALLY, so compute them:
   - `cameras[0]` is the establishing shot.  Pick the SUBJECT (the hero asset or the zone the
     brief is about) and its centre point S.  Put the camera at distance
     D = 1.5 x (subject radius) / tan(fov/2) from S, at height
     y = (tallest thing between camera and subject) + 0.30 x D, looking AT S (not at the bounds
     centre, not at the horizon).  It must see 2+ zones and be mostly content rather than empty
     sky/ground.
   - one eye-level camera INSIDE the scene at y = ground + 1.6-1.8 m, aimed along a path or
     across the subject so that something within 5 m frames one edge of the shot;
   - one detail camera 2-4 m from the hero asset, slightly above its mid height.
   - `purpose` must name the subject, the foreground framing element and what is on the horizon
     for that shot ("establishing: the arched bridge at 14 m, framed by the maple on the right at
     3 m, cedar ridge on the horizon").
   - No camera may be inside or up against geometry; fov 35-60 (vertical).
9. `acceptance` — 6 to 12 items covering: each must-have from the brief, the hero assets visible
   from the establishing camera, time-of-day lighting reads correctly, the hero motion is visible
   between t=0 and t=1.5 s, no floating or sunken assets (`how: probe`), no hard world edge from
   any authored camera.
   **`priority` matters more than anything else in this list.**  ANY unmet `must` item caps the
   whole run's score at 0.60, however good the pictures are — one over-specified detail throws
   away a passing scene.  So:
   - mark at most 4-6 items `must`, and only ones that restate a hard requirement of the brief
     itself, that a person can confirm from ONE rendered frame, and that name no incidental
     detail: "a raked gravel area is visible in the zen garden" is a `must`; "raked gravel with
     concentric rings around each rock" is a `should`;
   - `a1` is ALWAYS the premise: the kind of PLACE the brief describes, in the plainest words
     that would be false if the scene were somewhere else — "the view is from inside an
     enclosed workshop", "the courtyard is lit only by its lanterns at night".  Measured
     2026-08-25: a brief reading "INSIDE a boatbuilder's workshop, sun raking through a dusty
     window wall" was planned as "Coastal boatyard, clear dusk" and built faithfully as an
     outdoor yard with no walls and no roof — it passed every gate and scored 0.4653.  Write
     the premise down and the rest of the plan stays anchored to it — and when that premise
     is INSIDE a built space, set `interior: true`: the environment module then builds the
     enclosure on the bounds' faces (floor, walls, ceiling, with openings where `environment`
     puts the windows and doors) and the zones dress the inside.  Measured 2026-09-07 on
     six interior runs: with no owner for walls and roof, every one was judged "not
     enclosed, a diorama on a flat plane" (0.0–0.3) until a refine round built them.
     When the enclosure itself is glass on a frame (a conservatory, greenhouse, palm house,
     glass atrium) also set `glazed: true`: the walls and roof are then glass on an iron
     grid that the sun and the outside come through — an opaque room around a glass house
     was judged "a solid box blocking the exterior view and lighting";
   - mark everything else `priority: should` — craft goals, counts, specific patterns, exact
     heights and anything needing evidence the judge may not have;
   - never write a `must` whose wording contains a number, a named style, or the word "and"
     joining two independent claims — split it or demote it.

RULES
- Every name in `zones[].contents` must be an asset name; asset names unique; zone names unique.
- Realistic scale: people 1.7 m, doors 2.1 m, steps 0.17 m, benches 0.45 m seat height.
- Prefer <= 8 assets and 3-5 zones for modest briefs. No prose outside the JSON.

